WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:08.720
For centuries, doctors and scientists have
tried to unlock the mysteries of the human

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:09.840
brain.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:15.070
How is it organized?

00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:20.190
Which areas control different mental functions,
and how is it all wired together to generate

00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:23.620
our subjective psychological experience?

00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:28.980
For the past 100 years, neuroscientists have
approached the brain like map-makers, charting

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:32.960
its features and activities within well-defined
boundaries.

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:37.160
The prefrontal cortex is celebrated as the
seat of rationality.

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:40.269
The motor cortex plans and coordinates movement.

00:00:40.269 --> 00:00:46.079
The somatosensory cortex and parietal lobes
control our perception of the physical world.

00:00:46.079 --> 00:00:50.280
The temporal lobes process memories, language,
and emotion.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:54.050
The occipital lobe processes and integrates
visual information.

00:00:54.050 --> 00:00:58.609
And the cerebellum helps execute our body’s
motor commands.

00:00:58.609 --> 00:01:04.330
Scientists have looked at the brain through
the lens of our beliefs about what it means

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:05.630
to be human.

00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:10.501
So when we're looking at parts of the brain,
trying to figure out what they do, scientists

00:01:10.501 --> 00:01:15.280
are usually using their beliefs about what
makes a human mind, and some of those ideas

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:21.390
you can trace all the way back to Plato.

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:25.891
Recent studies of traditional categories of
cognitive brain functions, like memory, show

00:01:25.891 --> 00:01:31.290
surprising amounts of activity that overlap
different parts of the brain – so much that

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:36.329
the simple map and its strict categories lose
their meaning.

00:01:36.329 --> 00:01:43.990
The way that we've been conceptualizing the
mind doesn't map very well at all to the functions

00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:46.579
of different systems in the brain.

00:01:46.579 --> 00:01:52.220
These kinds of distinctions are very subjective
and it's probably better to try to look at

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:57.759
the brain and figure out what its organizational
properties are without appealing to these

00:01:57.759 --> 00:02:03.049
culturally ladened categories, which aren't
respected by the brain.

00:02:03.049 --> 00:02:05.439
Russell Poldrack is doing just that.

00:02:05.439 --> 00:02:10.250
At his lab at Stanford University, he’s
taking a computational approach to understanding

00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:12.760
the organizing features of our mind.

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:18.690
If you have people perform a bunch of different
psychological tasks that vary in the psychological

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:22.680
functions that we think they engage, let's
collect a bunch of data on a bunch of different

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:27.420
tasks and ask the data what kind of structure
there is.

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:31.920
Poldrack's lab used machine learning to try
to isolate neural activity related to memory

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:32.920
recall.

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:38.230
But rather than simply mapping into memory
centers, the data correlated with more general

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:42.640
constructs of activity — ones for which
we don't yet have names.

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:46.970
What that has shown us in some cases is that
things that we might've thought were measuring

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:50.800
the same thing really don't seem to be measuring
the same thing at all.

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:57.940
I think we need to fundamentally rethink how
we conceptualize the functions of the brain.

00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:03.510
Within neuroscience, there's a broad agreement
that the brain is a computational machine

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:06.930
and we need to understand
what the computations are that it's doing,

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:11.630
and that we should be able to ultimately understand
psychological function in terms of those computations.

00:03:11.630 --> 00:03:16.930
Part of the challenge is that we don’t have
a great language for describing those computations

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:17.930
other than math.

00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:21.930
So it raises essentially a question of whether
we're going to end up with models of the brain

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:28.400
that are really good at predicting the activity
of the brain, but that we can't sort of give

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:33.930
human understandable explanations to.

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:39.570
Deep in the jungles of Southeast Asia lives
the world’s strangest flower.

00:03:39.570 --> 00:03:41.300
This is Rafflesia arnoldii.

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:45.790
It’s affectionately known as the “corpse
flower” because it smells like rotting meat

00:03:45.790 --> 00:03:47.540
to attract pollinating flies.

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:53.840
It’s the largest flower in the world, with
the size and weight of a small child.

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:56.530
But the weirdness doesn’t end there.

00:03:56.530 --> 00:04:00.850
Rafflesia is not just a flower—it’s a
parasite.

00:04:00.850 --> 00:04:07.520
They require part or all of their nutrients
and water from another plant.

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:15.590
As a result, we often find alien genetic material
in the genome of a parasitic plant—many

00:04:15.590 --> 00:04:17.370
times from the host.

00:04:17.370 --> 00:04:26.180
One of the hypotheses is that parasitic plants
steal from the host as a weapon to make them

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:28.170
a better parasite.

00:04:28.170 --> 00:04:33.460
Liming Cai is the latest of a long line of
biologists to attempt to sequence Rafflesia’s

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:36.650
notoriously unwieldy genome.

00:04:36.650 --> 00:04:40.600
Biologists struggled because the Rafflesia
genome features highly repetitive elements

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:46.170
called transposons, known as jumping genes
for their ability to cut and paste themselves

00:04:46.170 --> 00:04:48.470
at repeating intervals.

00:04:48.470 --> 00:04:53.410
Most organisms silence these elements, but
Rafflesia is not most organisms.

00:04:53.410 --> 00:05:00.850
These highly repeated elements [are] causing
a lot of problems for a scientist trying to

00:05:00.850 --> 00:05:05.710
assemble genomes because it's basically like
putting a jigsaw puzzle, but every piece is

00:05:05.710 --> 00:05:07.680
identical.

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:12.889
This year, with the help of a bioinformatics
team, Cai successfully created a draft genome

00:05:12.889 --> 00:05:14.970
for a species of Rafflesia.

00:05:14.970 --> 00:05:18.060
Her findings were even more shocking than
biologists had expected.

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:20.210
There are a couple of things going on here
that really sort of blew my mind in the first

00:05:20.210 --> 00:05:21.610
place and really make us rethink what defines
a plant.

00:05:21.610 --> 00:05:25.030
So all plants have a similar set of genes.

00:05:25.030 --> 00:05:33.430
In Rafflesia, what we found is that it has
lost nearly half of the conserved plant genes,

00:05:33.430 --> 00:05:36.980
which is really a record-breaking finding.

00:05:36.980 --> 00:05:44.250
Cai also found that 90% of Rafflesia’s genome
consists of repeating DNA, like transposons.

00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:46.860
That’s highly unusual.

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:53.060
No one knows why, but the answer may transform
our understanding of parasite genomics.

00:05:53.060 --> 00:06:02.790
With the advances of genome sequencing technology,
we can explore all the weird branches of the

00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:12.260
tree of life—how rules can be bent by all
sorts of really creative strategies.

00:06:12.260 --> 00:06:22.960
Life is really diverse and nature often surprises.

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:27.800
In the early 20th century, sleep became a
popular topic for researchers.

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:34.680
The weapon of choice was the newly invented
electroencephalograph, or EEG, a machine measuring

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:37.330
electrical activity in the brain.

00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:42.330
This approach produced many insights, but
it also set up a bias in the studies: that

00:06:42.330 --> 00:06:48.880
sleep is a neurological phenomenon, and its
purpose and structure is located in the brain.

00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:52.200
Everybody has thought that sleep is of the
brain, by the brain, for the brain.

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:57.260
This is a famous quote by Alan Hobson, who's
a brilliant sleep scientist that made some

00:06:57.260 --> 00:06:58.330
huge contributions to the field.

00:06:58.330 --> 00:07:02.360
But it really overlooks the fact that, in
fact, we're not brains.

00:07:02.360 --> 00:07:05.590
We’re organisms, we’re integrated.

00:07:05.590 --> 00:07:07.920
Everything we do is integrated with everything
else.

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:12.520
The first cracks in this brain-centric view
started to show when the Swiss scientist Irene

00:07:12.520 --> 00:07:15.670
Tobler noticed that cockroaches sleep.

00:07:15.670 --> 00:07:21.550
Since then, we’ve learned that simpler creatures,
with less and less brain, also sleep.

00:07:21.550 --> 00:07:27.220
And recently, a new discovery has changed
the narrative entirely.

00:07:27.220 --> 00:07:28.550
This is a hydra.

00:07:28.550 --> 00:07:32.180
It’s one of the simplest forms of animal
life.

00:07:32.180 --> 00:07:38.070
Instead of a brain, hydras have nerve nets,
the most basic nervous systems in nature.

00:07:38.070 --> 00:07:41.990
This year, a group of Japanese scientists
demonstrated that hydras sleep.

00:07:41.990 --> 00:07:46.860
These tiny fresh-water organisms are living
proof that sleep evolved before brains.

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:51.040
But if sleep didn’t evolve in and for the
brain, what is it for?

00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:55.360
More and more scientists are really looking
in peripheral tissues and asking how the body

00:07:55.360 --> 00:07:59.250
can impact the brain and how the brain can
impact the body specifically with respect

00:07:59.250 --> 00:08:00.340
to sleep regulation.

00:08:00.340 --> 00:08:04.710
In my lab, the current hypothesis is that
there are some situations that the brain can't

00:08:04.710 --> 00:08:05.710
fix itself.

00:08:05.710 --> 00:08:07.260
And in association with whatever damage has
taken place, sleep can lower the activation

00:08:07.260 --> 00:08:10.979
energy to have these circuits begin to find
that solution.

00:08:10.979 --> 00:08:15.150
So the idea then is that when you're asleep,
you're using less energy, but the energy that

00:08:15.150 --> 00:08:18.930
you are using, you're using in a different
way that you're supporting functions that

00:08:18.930 --> 00:08:21.920
you would not be able to otherwise support
if you were awake.

00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:26.350
The research on hydras is the latest in a
growing body of evidence that sleep first

00:08:26.350 --> 00:08:32.330
evolved to help regulate metabolism and enhance
repair, and only later took on brain-related

00:08:32.330 --> 00:08:33.330
functions.

00:08:33.330 --> 00:08:35.680
I really do believe that sleep and metabolism
are intertwined.

00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:50.620
So that's going to be the future.

